    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We start by using the proportionality relationship between $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$. Since $\\alpha$ is inversely proportional to $\\beta$, we can write the relationship as\n\n$\\alpha \\cdot \\beta = k,$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nWe are given that $\\alpha = 4$ when $\\beta = 9$. Substituting these values into the equation above, we can solve for $k$:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n4 \\cdot 9 &= k \\\\\n\\times \\frac{1}{(4 \\cdot 9)} = 1 \\\\\n\\implies k &= 36.\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow, we can use this value of $k$ to find $\\alpha$ when $\\beta = -72$:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\alpha \\cdot (-72) &= k \\\\\n\\implies \\alpha &= \\frac{k}{-72} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{(-72)} \\\\\n&= \\boxed{-\\frac{1}{2}}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nThe answer is: -\\frac{1}{2}",
